---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Every fact and number here is on the resume and its evidence ledger (docs/02-writing-plan.md, Bio sources).
const EMAIL = 'shivamjaiswal82@gmail.com';
const LINKEDIN_URL = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/shivam-jaiswal-556059170';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About · Shivam Jaiswal"
  description="Full-stack engineer at BNY and solo builder of Tracktions: experience, projects and how I work."
>
  <article class="article prose-column">
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">about</p>
      <h1 class="display">
        Shivam Jaiswal
        <span class="display-quiet">full-stack engineer, India, open to fully remote</span>
      </h1>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I've spent four years as a software engineer at BNY on an enterprise data platform: Java and
        Spring Boot services, ingestion pipelines, a config compiler, test automation and internal AI
        tooling, and I now lead a four-person frontend team. Outside work I designed, built and run a
        whole product alone, and I'm building a trading engine where every failure has a written
        price.
      </p>
      <p>
        I work from written decisions. Before the code there's a doc: the options and why the loser
        lost, what each part does when it breaks, which numbers are measured and which are only
        assumed. I care about removing moving parts as much as adding them.
      </p>

      <h2>BNY · software engineer · 2022 to now</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Made a changing codebase safe to change.</strong> Led four frontend engineers on an
          Angular codebase with <code>any</code> everywhere, no lint rules and no review rules. Replaced
          <code>any</code> with strict typing, turned the standards into lint rules so they held without
          policing, and made merge-request review and CI gates mandatory. Over six months defects
          fell from one or two a sprint to one or two a quarter, while the team kept shipping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turned hand-written config into a UI.</strong> Built the process that flattens the
          platform's tree-structured data into generated JSON config, which drives table creation and
          the whole ingestion pipeline. Then designed a visual builder for multi-table joins and wrote
          the compiler that emits the same config.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Automated acceptance testing with LLM agents.</strong> Built the Java test framework,
          then replaced its manual verification step with a two-agent pipeline that runs end-to-end
          acceptance tests across 30+ datasets and 50+ microservices, driven by a scenario-based
          validation language with seven validation types.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Internal AI developer platform, in an air-gapped network.</strong> Shipped a
          marketplace for AI skills and MCP servers with one-click install and an open contribution
          model, then folded it into one developer MCP server so a single install exposes the whole
          catalogue. Nine skills and eight workflows, used by 18 developers across three teams.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>2022 to 2024:</strong> sole developer of a Spring Boot project from schema to UI, and
          owner of production debugging for the platform.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tracktions · founder and sole engineer · Oct 2024 to now</h2>
      <p>
        A trading journal for Indian retail traders, live at
        <a href="https://tracktions.com">tracktions.com</a>. I chose the market and the pricing, and I
        run every layer: a Go API on PostgreSQL, an Angular client, broker sync and file import, AWS
        hosting and subscription billing. Its design pack is written before the code: principles,
        flows, algorithm specs, a failure table, an assumptions register and 13 decision records.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/posts/analytics-from-the-record/">Delete the running totals</a>: metrics computed on read from trade facts, benchmarked at 100,000 trades before any code.</li>
        <li><a href="/posts/three-job-queues/">The job queue I built three times</a>: Redis, Go channels, then a Postgres table.</li>
        <li><a href="/posts/a-number-the-user-cant-misread/">A number the user can't misread</a>: sample sizes, provenance, and staying a calculator rather than an adviser.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>artha-engine · design and build · 2026</h2>
      <p>
        An event-driven options trading engine in Python with a React dashboard. Its design starts
        from one rule: no failure may cost more than a limit set before the order is sent. Every
        failure has a row with its detection, response, worst case and test. It ran its first paper
        sessions in October 2026, and those sessions proved several of my own estimates wrong.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/posts/every-failure-has-a-price/">Every failure has a price</a></li>
        <li><a href="/posts/one-order-manager-per-account/">One engine, one order manager per account</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Public work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/shivamjaiswal011/codebase-context-pilot">codebase-context-pilot</a>:
          a controlled 10-task A/B test of a code-graph context system for coding agents. I fixed the
          pass/fail rule before collecting data, found two measurement defects that invalidated the
          first pass, and published the negative result instead of shipping the tool.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/shivamjaiswal011/tracktions-architecture">tracktions-architecture</a>:
          the first public notes on Tracktions' architecture. The posts above update them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/shivamjaiswal011/health-app">health-app</a>: an offline-first
          training and nutrition tracker in TypeScript. No server, no account, and the data stays on
          the device.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://github.com/shivamjaiswal011/Competitive-Coding-From-scratch">Competitive-Coding-From-scratch</a>:
          a topic-by-topic competitive programming curriculum in C++, with 118 stars and 30 forks.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tools</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Languages:</strong> Java, TypeScript, Go, Python, SQL.
        <strong>Frontend:</strong> Angular, React, RxJS, Tailwind, ag-Grid, Chart.js.
        <strong>Backend and data:</strong> Spring Boot, Gin, PostgreSQL, REST, server-sent events.
        <strong>Infrastructure:</strong> AWS (EC2, RDS, S3, SES), Docker, GitLab and GitHub CI.
        <strong>AI tooling:</strong> MCP servers, multi-agent LLM pipelines, Claude Code.
      </p>
      <p>B.Tech in Computer Engineering, Delhi Technological University, 2018 to 2022.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a> ·
        <a href="https://github.com/shivamjaiswal011">GitHub</a> ·
        <a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>
      </p>
      <p>The source for Tracktions and artha-engine is private. I'm happy to walk through it on a call.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
